From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bringup <bringup@t1100.local>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:49:54 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] usb: mtu3: log the UDC name once it is registered

udc-core registers the class device silently, so a boot log from a board whose
only debug channel is printk/pstore cannot distinguish "no UDC" from "UDC
registered, no gadget driver bound yet". That ambiguity already cost a boot
cycle on the T1100, where mtu3 probe ends with "dma mask: 36 bits" and the
next USB line in the log is the shutdown of the same device.

Print the UDC name (what /sys/class/udc/<name> will be) on success and keep
the error path explicit.
---
 drivers/usb/mtu3/mtu3_gadget.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/mtu3/mtu3_gadget.c b/drivers/usb/mtu3/mtu3_gadget.c
index f224f2ee3..519c4084f 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/mtu3/mtu3_gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/mtu3/mtu3_gadget.c
@@ -707,6 +707,8 @@ static void mtu3_gadget_init_eps(struct mtu3 *mtu)
 
 int mtu3_gadget_setup(struct mtu3 *mtu)
 {
+	int ret;
+
 	mtu->g.ops = &mtu3_gadget_ops;
 	mtu->g.max_speed = mtu->max_speed;
 	mtu->g.speed = USB_SPEED_UNKNOWN;
@@ -718,7 +720,18 @@ int mtu3_gadget_setup(struct mtu3 *mtu)
 
 	mtu3_gadget_init_eps(mtu);
 
-	return usb_add_gadget_udc(mtu->dev, &mtu->g);
+	ret = usb_add_gadget_udc(mtu->dev, &mtu->g);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(mtu->dev, "failed to register UDC: %d\n", ret);
+	else
+		/*
+		 * udc-core is silent on success, so without this line a log
+		 * from a board with no other debug channel cannot tell a working
+		 * UDC from a missing one. One line per controller is cheap.
+		 */
+		dev_info(mtu->dev, "UDC '%s' registered\n", mtu->g.name);
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 void mtu3_gadget_cleanup(struct mtu3 *mtu)
